{
  "id": "in-place-prefix-sum",
  "title": "In-Place Prefix Sum",
  "subjectId": "intermediate-dsa-1",
  "nextLessonId": "range-sum-query",
  "prevLessonId": "introduction-to-prefix-sum",
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Welcome back! In our last lesson, we learned about standard Prefix Sums. We saw how creating a separate prefix sum array lets us query any range sum in O(1) constant time. But did you notice a small drawback? We had to allocate an extra array of the same size, which takes O(N) extra memory space. Today, we'll learn a clever optimization: how to build the prefix sum directly **in-place** within the original array to achieve O(1) auxiliary space complexity!"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "what-is-in-place-prefix-sum",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "1. What is In-Place Prefix Sum?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "First, let's understand what **in-place** means. In-place means we modify our data structure directly without allocating a new one. In our case, we calculate prefix sums by overwriting the values of the original array step-by-step. By the end of the process, the original array itself has been transformed into the prefix sum array, saving us memory space."
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "id": "in-place-prefix-sum-intro-drawing"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "id": "how-to-build-it",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "2. How to Build It"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "How do we do this? We can start our loop from index `1`. We add the value from the previous index `arr[i - 1]` to the current element `arr[i]`, and overwrite `arr[i]` with this sum. This matches our recurrence relation!"
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "style": "important",
      "text": "Formula to build in-place prefix sum:\n`arr[i] = arr[i - 1] + arr[i]`  (for i >= 1)"
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "id": "in-place-prefix-sum-building-drawing"
    },
    {
